.Op Fl f
.Cm chmod
.Ar image
.Ar mode
.Ar path ...
.Nm
.Op Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
.Op Fl f
.Cm chown
.Ar image
.Ar uid : Ns Ar gid
.Ar path ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads a MINIX file system image of any version, V1, V2 or V3, in either
byte order, and of Minix-vmd; see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
The commands from
.Cm put
on change the image, as the mtools change an MS-DOS image, without
mounting it; the others never write to it.
Paths inside the image are taken from its root directory whether or not
they start with
.Ql / .
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm info
Print the super block: version, byte order, magic number, name length,
block size, the numbers of inodes and zones, the sizes of the bit maps,
the first data zone, the zone size, the maximum file size, whether the
file system is marked clean, and for Minix-vmd the variant.
Then the size of the image against that of the file system, and how
much of the image is sectors of nothing but 0xe5 or 0xa5, which formats
leave and nothing else writes, with the commonest length of such runs.
A disk read with more sides than it was written on shows as runs of one
track making up half of the image; a copy cut short, or a file system
smaller than its disk, shows too.
None of this is an error in itself.
The size of a device is asked of the system, and shown as unknown where
it cannot be told; a device is read for such sectors only as far as the
file system.
Last come the free inodes and zones that the bit maps count.
.It Cm ls Oo Fl lR Oc Ar image Op Ar path
List the directory
.Ar path ,
the root by default, or the file that
.Ar path
names.
With
.Fl l ,
each entry shows its mode, link count, owner, group, size (or major and
minor numbers for devices) and modification time in UTC, and a symbolic
link its target.
.Fl R
lists subdirectories as well.
.It Cm cat Ar image Ar path
Copy the regular file
.Ar path
to standard output.
.It Cm blocks Oo Fl r Oc Ar image Ar path
Print the blocks that hold the regular file, directory or symbolic link
.Ar path ,
in the order of the file and up to its size: a line for each block of
the file, with its number in the file and the number of the block, or
.Ql -
for a hole.
With
.Fl r ,
blocks that follow one another come as runs, the first block and the
count, in the order of the file; a run of holes has
.Ql -
for its first block.
Blocks are of the block size that
.Cm info
gives, counted from the start of the file system, which with
.Fl M
is not that of
.Ar image .
The last block may be in use in part only.
.It Cm extract Oo Fl dv Oc Ar image Ar dest Op Ar path
Copy the directory
.Ar path ,
the root by default, and all below it into the directory
.Ar dest ,
which is made if it does not exist.
Files, directories, symbolic links and pipes are made with their
permission bits, without the set-user-ID, set-group-ID and sticky bits,
and their times; owners are not set.
The other names of a file with more than one link are made as links to
the first.
Devices are made only with
.Fl d ,
which takes root.
Devices left out and sockets, which cannot be copied, are counted in a
warning at the end, and with
.Fl v
named one by one as all that is copied is.
A name that is already in
.Ar dest
is replaced, as
.Xr tar 1
replaces it: what is there is removed first, unless it is a directory,
so that nothing is written through another link, a pipe or a file
without write permission.
The disks of a set thus go one after the other into one tree.
.Pp
.Cm extract
never writes outside
.Ar dest :
entries whose names are empty,
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql \&..
or contain
.Ql /
are refused, and so are directories that contain themselves.
.It Cm tar Ar image Op Ar path
Write the directory
.Ar path ,
the root by default, to standard output as a POSIX ustar archive, which
must not be a terminal.
Besides pipes and hard links, it keeps, unlike
.Cm extract ,
devices with their numbers and owners, which
.Xr tar 1
lists without privileges and makes when run as root.
Names and link targets longer than ustar holds go into pax extended
headers.
Sockets cannot be stored and are counted as left out.
.It Cm put Oo Fl Rdin Oc Oo Fl o Ar uid : Ns Ar gid Oc Ar image Ar host ... Ar path
Copy files of the host into the image, as
.Cm extract
copies them out: into the directory
.Ar path
under their own names, or as
.Ar path
if that is not a directory yet.
Regular files, symbolic links and pipes go in with their modes and
mtimes; directories with
.Fl R ,
and what is in them; devices with
.Fl d
only.
Sockets cannot go in, and are counted in a warning at the end with the
devices left out.
A name in the way is replaced, unless
.Fl n
keeps it, or
.Fl i
asks first, on standard error, and takes an answer from standard input;
a directory in the way of a directory is entered.
A file that runs out of room is removed again.
.It Cm mkdir Oo Fl p Oc Oo Fl m Ar mode Oc Oo Fl o Ar uid : Ns Ar gid Oc Ar image Ar path ...
Make directories, of mode 0755 unless
.Fl m
gives one in octal; with
.Fl p ,
each missing directory on the way as well, of mode 0755, and no
complaint about one that is there.
.It Cm rm Oo Fl r Oc Ar image Ar path ...
Remove files, and with
.Fl r
directories and everything in them.
.It Cm mv Ar image Ar old Ar new
Rename
.Ar old
to
.Ar new ,
or move it into
.Ar new
if that is a directory, in place of a file of that name, or of an empty
directory in place of a directory.
.It Cm ln Oo Fl s Oc Ar image Ar target Ar path
Give the file
.Ar target
another name,
.Ar path ,
or with
.Fl s
make
.Ar path
a symbolic link to the text
.Ar target ;
into the directory
.Ar path
under the last component of
.Ar target
if
.Ar path
is one.
.It Cm chmod Ar image Ar mode Ar path ...
Set the permission bits, in octal.
.It Cm chown Ar image Ar uid : Ns Ar gid Ar path ...
Set the owner, the group, or both, as numbers:
.Ar uid ,
.Ar uid : Ns Ar gid
or
.Cm \&: Ns Ar gid ;
the image knows no names.
.El
.Pp
What the commands make belongs to the directory it is made in, since the
owner of a file in an image is a matter of the system that will use the
image, not of who runs
.Nm ;
.Fl o
gives another owner, with numbers that the inodes can hold (a group of
a byte in V1).
The image is locked while a command has it open, as
.Nm mount_minixfs Fl w
locks it, and its clean mark is away until the command is done, so that
one cut short leaves it for
.Nm fsck_minixfs .
A file system that is not marked clean may be mounted elsewhere and is
refused, unless
.Fl f
is given; the flex directories of Minix-vmd, which cannot be written,
are refused always.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
Read an image that holds the file system in the tracks of one side of a
disk only: tracks of
.Ar size
bytes,
.Ar heads
of them to a cylinder, of which side
.Ar side ,
from 0, holds the file system.
A single-sided 360K disk read as a double-sided 720K one, as are some
Atari MINIX 1.5 disks, is
.Fl M Ar 4608:2:0 :
every other track of the image is empty.
The super block of such an image is still in place, so
.Cm info
looks right while the rest reads as damage; its fill count shows half of
the image as runs of 4608 bytes.
.It Fl f
Let the commands that write change a file system that is not marked
clean, which is left as it is.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 on success, 1 if anything failed, and 2 for a usage error.
Each command reports every problem it meets and goes on where it can.
.Sh EXAMPLES
List an image and copy one file out of it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ minixfs ls -l root.img /etc
$ minixfs cat root.img /etc/passwd > passwd
.Ed
.Pp
Find where the boot program lies, as installboot of MINIX writes it into
the boot block:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ minixfs blocks -r root.img /boot
.Ed
.Pp
Put a tree into an image, and a file over one that is there, asking
first:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ minixfs put -R usr.img src /usr
$ minixfs put -i usr.img passwd /etc/passwd
.Ed
.Pp
Move the whole tree, devices and hard links included, onto a new image:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# mkdir tree && minixfs tar old.img | tar -xpf - -C tree
# newfs_minixfs -V 3 -d tree new.img
.Ed
.Pp
Unpack the two
.Pa /usr
disks of a set into one tree:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ minixfs extract usr1.img usr && minixfs extract usr2.img usr
.Ed
.Pp
Read an Atari disk that was read as double-sided:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ minixfs -M 4608:2:0 extract disk02 out
.Ed
